1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:07,017
Your graphics card has about sixteen milliseconds to draw every frame you see at sixty frames per second.

2
00:00:07,297 --> 00:00:17,349
In this video, we'll follow one single frame through the GPU, from a pile of triangles to a finished picture, and see exactly how it pulls that off.

3
00:00:18,099 --> 00:00:19,263
Here's the job.

4
00:00:19,543 --> 00:00:23,280
A full HD screen has just over two million pixels.

5
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:29,301
Every one of them needs a color, and every one of them has to be ready before the next frame is due.

6
00:00:29,581 --> 00:00:38,141
Do the math, and that's more than a hundred million pixel colors every second, before the game even gets fancy with lighting or shadows.

7
00:00:38,421 --> 00:00:44,114
At 4K, it's about four times as much, close to five hundred million every second.

8
00:00:44,394 --> 00:00:47,593
No ordinary processor can keep up with that alone.

9
00:00:47,873 --> 00:00:51,465
So the GPU isn't built like an ordinary processor.

10
00:00:51,745 --> 00:00:54,803
Let's watch how it's built, by following our frame.

11
00:00:55,553 --> 00:00:57,541
Our frame starts on the CPU.

12
00:00:57,821 --> 00:01:02,954
The game works out where the camera is, where the characters stand, and what's on screen.

13
00:01:03,234 --> 00:01:07,736
Then it hands the graphics card a list of instructions called draw calls.

14
00:01:08,016 --> 00:01:12,778
Each one says, roughly, draw this model, with these textures, at this position.

15
00:01:13,058 --> 00:01:14,826
The CPU is the director.

16
00:01:15,106 --> 00:01:18,352
The GPU is the crew that actually builds the set.

17
00:01:19,102 --> 00:01:21,524
The first thing the GPU builds is shape.

18
00:01:21,804 --> 00:01:29,715
Every 3D model in a game is made of triangles, and each triangle is defined by three corners called vertices.

19
00:01:29,995 --> 00:01:40,169
A small program called a vertex shader runs on every single vertex, moving it from the model's own space into the world, and then onto your screen.

20
00:01:40,449 --> 00:01:48,905
A character might have tens of thousands of vertices, and they're all processed in parallel, because no vertex needs to wait for another.

21
00:01:49,655 --> 00:01:51,414
Next comes rasterization.

22
00:01:51,694 --> 00:01:59,025
The GPU takes each triangle, now flattened onto the screen, and asks a simple question for every pixel.

23
00:01:59,305 --> 00:02:02,133
Is this pixel inside the triangle or not?

24
00:02:02,413 --> 00:02:07,303
The pixels it covers become fragments, which are candidates for a final color.

25
00:02:07,583 --> 00:02:15,440
Triangles facing away from the camera are thrown out early, so the GPU never wastes time on the back of a character's head.

26
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:20,112
Even one small triangle on screen can cover dozens of pixels.

27
00:02:20,392 --> 00:02:25,424
A whole scene covers millions, and every one of them is now waiting to be colored.

28
00:02:26,174 --> 00:02:27,478
Now the heavy lifting.

29
00:02:27,758 --> 00:02:31,053
For each fragment, a pixel shader works out the color.

30
00:02:31,333 --> 00:02:36,558
It reads the texture, the image wrapped around the model, from the card's video memory.

31
00:02:36,838 --> 00:02:41,579
It checks where the lights are, how shiny the surface is, and whether it's in shadow.

32
00:02:41,859 --> 00:02:45,503
And it has to do this for millions of fragments, every frame.

33
00:02:45,783 --> 00:02:49,967
The good news is that every fragment's color can be worked out on its own.

34
00:02:50,247 --> 00:02:55,534
The pixel on a character's nose doesn't need to know about the pixel on the tree behind them.

35
00:02:55,814 --> 00:02:59,584
This is the moment the GPU's design really pays off.

36
00:03:00,334 --> 00:03:11,863
Inside a GPU like the GeForce RTX 40 60, there are 24 blocks called streaming multiprocessors, each with 128 small cores.

37
00:03:12,143 --> 00:03:14,879
That's 3,072 cores in total.

38
00:03:15,159 --> 00:03:16,736
But here's the clever part.

39
00:03:17,016 --> 00:03:18,817
Those cores don't work alone.

40
00:03:19,097 --> 00:03:29,273
They're grouped into teams of 32 threads called warps, and every thread in a warp runs the same instruction at the same moment, just on different pixels.

41
00:03:29,553 --> 00:03:31,855
One instruction, thirty-two pixels.

42
00:03:32,135 --> 00:03:39,266
That's how a chip keeps millions of shader calculations moving without needing millions of separate control units.

43
00:03:40,016 --> 00:03:41,200
There's a catch, though.

44
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:45,247
Reading a texture from memory is slow compared to doing math.

45
00:03:45,527 --> 00:03:52,534
So when one warp is stuck waiting for data, the multiprocessor simply switches to another warp that's ready to go.

46
00:03:52,814 --> 00:03:57,881
Each warp's state stays in the chip's registers, so switching costs almost nothing.

47
00:03:58,161 --> 00:04:03,964
The GPU hides the wait by always having more work lined up than it can run at once.

48
00:04:04,244 --> 00:04:14,252
And if threads in the same warp take different branches, say half the pixels are in shadow and half aren't, the warp has to run both paths one after the other.

49
00:04:14,532 --> 00:04:17,932
That's one reason some effects cost more than you'd expect.

50
00:04:18,212 --> 00:04:24,723
Graphics programmers spend a lot of effort keeping neighboring pixels on the same path, so the warps stay busy.

51
00:04:25,473 --> 00:04:27,730
All of this needs a firehose of data.

52
00:04:28,010 --> 00:04:38,538
The RTX 40 60 has 8 gigabytes of video memory on a 128 bit bus, moving about 272 gigabytes per second.

53
00:04:38,817 --> 00:04:48,017
It also has 24 megabytes of level two cache on the chip, so the textures it uses most don't have to make the trip to memory every time.

54
00:04:48,297 --> 00:04:55,827
When a game needs more textures than the video memory can hold, that's when you see stutter, and that's a story for another video.

55
00:04:56,577 --> 00:04:59,393
Modern cards add two specialists to the crew.

56
00:04:59,673 --> 00:05:10,728
The RTX 40 60 has 24 ray tracing cores, built to quickly test which objects a ray of light would hit, for realistic reflections and shadows.

57
00:05:11,008 --> 00:05:22,609
And it has 96 tensor cores, built for the kind of math that AI uses, which powers upscaling features that draw a smaller image and rebuild it at a higher resolution.

58
00:05:22,889 --> 00:05:24,760
They don't replace the main cores.

59
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:31,275
They take the jobs the main cores are worst at, so the regular shaders are free for everything else.

60
00:05:32,025 --> 00:05:38,271
Finally, the finished colors land in a frame buffer, a block of memory holding the complete image.

61
00:05:38,551 --> 00:05:44,256
While you're looking at the previous frame, the GPU is filling in the next one behind the scenes.

62
00:05:44,536 --> 00:05:48,852
When it's done, the two swap, and the new picture goes out to your monitor.

63
00:05:49,132 --> 00:05:53,011
Then the whole thing starts again, sixty or more times a second.

64
00:05:53,291 --> 00:05:59,672
If any step runs late, the frame misses its deadline, and that's what you feel as a lower frame rate.

65
00:05:59,952 --> 00:06:03,231
Whichever stage is slowest sets the pace for everything.

66
00:06:03,981 --> 00:06:05,308
So that's one frame.

67
00:06:05,588 --> 00:06:22,519
The CPU directs, vertex shaders place the triangles, rasterization finds the pixels, warps of thirty-two shade them together, memory feeds the whole thing, and the finished image swaps onto your screen, all in about sixteen milliseconds.

68
00:06:22,799 --> 00:06:28,401
Of course, all those textures and models live in memory, and not all memory is the same.

69
00:06:28,681 --> 00:06:36,701
Next time, we'll go through every type of RAM, from DDR3 to DDR5, and what actually changed.

70
00:06:36,981 --> 00:06:39,458
If this helped, subscribe so you don't miss it.

